==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|up to}}, from {{inh|en|ang|up tō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌp tuː/|/ˈʌp tə/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-up to.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈʌp tə/|a=GA}}

===Preposition===
{{en-head|prep}}

# {{&lit|en|up|to}}
#: {{ux|en|She went '''up to''' the third floor.}}
#: {{ux|en|Go '''up to''' the counter and ask.}}
#* {{RQ:Baum Wizard of Oz|page=21|passage=But the little old woman walked '''up to''' Dorothy, made a low bow and said, in a sweet voice, “You are welcome, most noble Sorceress, to the land of the Munchkins.{{nb...}}”}}
# [[capable|Capable]], [[ready]] or [[equipped]], having [[sufficient]] [[material]] [[precondition]]s for, possibly [[willpower]] (at a particular [[moment]]).
#: {{syn|en|equal|}}
#: {{coi|en|to feel '''up to''' it}}
#: {{ux|en|Are you '''up to''' lifting something that heavy?}}
#: {{ux|en|Are you '''up to''' the challenge?}}
## [[willing|Willing]], [[interested]] in
##: {{ux|en|Are you '''up to''' going to the beach?}}
# [[as much as|As much as]]; no more than {{lb|en|also with ''[[of]]''}}.
#: {{ux|en|You can make '''up to''' five copies.}}
#: {{ux|en|Violators may face a fine of '''up to''' $300.}}
# [[until|Until]].
#: {{syn|en|up until}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Up to''' that point, I liked her.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've read '''up to''' chapter 5.}}
# Within the [[responsibility]] of, to be [[attributed]] to the [[sphere of influence]] of, having someone or something as [[authoritative]] in.
#: {{syn|en|down to}}
#: {{hypo|en|[[incumbent]] upon|the [[obligation]] of|the [[duty]] of|the [[decision]] of}}
#: {{ux|en|It’s '''up to''' you whether to get the blue one or the red one.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's '''up to''' the prosecution to prove that the defendant is guilty.}}
# [[do|Doing]], [[involved]] in.
#: {{coi|en|to be '''up to'''}}
#: {{ux|en|I have no idea what they've been [[get up to|getting '''up to''']] while we've been away.}}
#: {{ux|en|What are you '''up to''' today?}}
## [[devise|Devising]], [[scheme|scheming]] {{gloss|planning something mischievous or inappropriate}}.
##: {{ux|en|He looked like a man '''up to''' no good.}}
##: {{ux|en|What are you kids '''up to''' with all that toilet paper?}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} Considering all [[member]]s of an [[equivalence class]] the same.{{wp}}
#: {{ux|en|There’s only one rooted tree with two leaves, '''up to''' ordering.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|slang}} [[familiar|Familiar]] with the [[meaning]] of; able to [[understand]].
#* {{RQ:Dickens Pickwick Papers|chapter=Illustrative, like the preceding one, of the old Proverb, that Adversity brings a Man acquainted with strange Bed-fellows. Likewise containing Mr. Pickwick’s extraordinary and startling announcement to Mr. Samuel Weller.|page=447|passage=“Oh,” said Mr. Pickwick. “My what, did you say?”<br>“Your chummage ticket,” replied Mr. Roker; “you’re '''up to''' that?”<br>“Not quite,” replied Mr. Pickwick, with a smile.<br>“Why,” said Mr. Roker, “it’s as plain as Salisbury. You’ll have a chummage ticket upon twenty-seven in the third, and them as is in the room will be your chums.”}}
# {{lb|en|Eton College}} Taught by; in the class of.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1899|author=Alfred Lubbock; Robin Lubbock|title=Memories of Eton and Etonians|page=62
|passage=I was '''up to''' Mr. Wayte, and, although he was a fairly easy and by no means strict master, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022|author=Maurice Baring|title=The Puppet Show of Memory
|passage=I was '''up to''' Mr. Luxmoore, who was to be a lifelong friend. The last days of my last half were like a dream. I was hardly conscious of the reality of things, and I did not yet fully realise that my Eton life was coming to an end {{...}}}}

